{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Fill your hands today.</b> Each and every hand of yours has already been filled, for you have become servants of HaKadosh Baruch Hu, since <b>each man against his son and against his brother</b> — you stretched out your hands for the sake of Heaven, and so the Targum implies. And one cannot say 'fill your hands' for the future, for they had already done according to Moses' words." "<b>And to bestow upon you a blessing today.</b> From here the Levites merited the blessing of Moses in the portion of the blessing, even though Jacob did not bless them; and the Reubenites merited through crossing the Jordan armed at his command. But the Simeonites did not merit his blessing, because they acted corruptly with the Midianite woman and disparaged Moses, saying to him: the daughter of Jethro — who permitted her to you? Another interpretation: <b>and to bestow upon you a blessing today</b> — this is what is said in the portion of Ekev: 'at that time HaShem set apart' etc., 'and to bless in His name unto this day.' In that passage it was decreed that they would be set apart, and when the Tabernacle was erected, what had already been decreed was completed, and Aaron and his sons were set apart to serve HaKadosh Baruch Hu. The Levites too were taken in place of the firstborn, because they offered upon the altar that was made before the calf. At that time the dispute of Korah began, for he wanted to perform divine service, because he too was a firstborn, as it is written: 'and the sons of Yitshar: Korah, Nepheg, and Zichri.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:01:37.516415923Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}